To definitively overcome the aforementioned limitations plaguing active learning for ICL, we propose a groundbreaking synthesis that seamlessly pairs uncertainty-based sampling with diversity-based sampling. To optimally orchestrate this powerful combination, we introduce a novel, model-adaptive, and entirely optimization-free method, termed \adaicl, which is meticulously tailored for retrieval-based $k$-shot ICL. \adaicl leverages the LLM's intrinsic feedback—specifically its output probability distributions—to precisely identify the examples that elicit the highest degree of model uncertainty, which we designate as \emph{hard examples}. The algorithm then proceeds to delineate distinct semantic regions within this set of hard examples, with the strategic objective of selecting the most representative exemplar from each region. We innovatively cast this diversity-based sampling objective as the well-studied Maximum Coverage problem (\mcp), a formulation that admits efficient and high-quality approximate solutions via established greedy algorithms.

By strategically selecting representative examples from diverse hard regions, \adaicl is architected to achieve maximal \emph{effectiveness}, ensuring it guides the LLM towards information it does not already possess. Simultaneously, \adaicl is engineered for exceptional \emph{efficiency}, realizing significant budget savings by deliberately omitting examples that the model can already handle with ease (easy examples). Furthermore, we demonstrate that \adaicl's principled uncertainty sampling directly enhances the LLM's calibration~\citep{jiang2021can, zhao2021calibrate}—that is, the critical alignment between the model's expressed confidence and its true epistemic uncertainty, which serves as a fundamental measure of how thoroughly the model comprehends the task at hand.

We conduct an exhaustive and rigorous suite of experiments across nine challenging datasets, spanning five core NLP tasks (topic classification, sentiment analysis, natural language inference, summarization, and math reasoning), and evaluate with seven premier LLMs of varying scales (from 1.3B to 65B parameters), including flagship models such as GPT-J~\citep{wang2021gpt-j}, Mosaic~\citep{MosaicML2023Introducing}, Falcon~\citep{penedo2023falcon}, and LLaMa~\citep{touvron2023llama}. The experimental results provide irrefutable validation of our approach, demonstrating that (i) \adaicl is profoundly effective, achieving an average performance improvement of 4.4\% accuracy points over the current state-of-the-art (as vividly illustrated in \Figref{fig:ada_intro}), (ii) \adaicl is exceptionally robust and efficient, securing up to $3\times$ greater budget savings compared to random annotation, while requiring only half ($2 \times$ fewer) the ICL examples needed by SOTA methods to outperform them (\Secref{sec:res2}), and (iii) \adaicl consistently and significantly improves the calibration of the LLM's predictions (\Secref{sec:res-calib}).",
