\section{Analysis of Compositional Abilities}



\textbf{Systematic Generalization: Composition over in-context skills},  where all the needed skills are provided in the context. We evaluate (i) last letter concatenation \citep{wei2022chain,zhou2022least,khot2022decomposed}, where the LLM needs to generate the concatenation of the last letter from a given list of words, (ii) addition and multiplication \citep{dziri2023faith}, where the LLM needs to generate the sum and product of two numbers, (iii) CommaQA-E \citep{khot2022decomposed}, where models need to answer multi-hop questions, and (iv) dynamic programming \citep{dziri2023faith}, where LLMs need to find the highest sum for a subsequence where no two numbers are adjacent. These tasks require only a limited skill set and we construct SKiC prompts manually in Figures~\ref{Tab:last_letter_skill}-\ref{Tab:compose_dp_skill}, with similar human effort as in CoT prompting.

\textbf{Complex Reasoning: Generalization beyond in-context skills},  where models need to harness skills beyond the context and tap into the internal skills for math reasoning like GSM8K~\citep{cobbe2021training} and MATH~\citep{hendrycks2021measuring}. 
For GSM8K, which are simpler problems that could be solved by basic math operations, we construct SKiC via human in Figures~\ref{Tab:gsm8k_skill}-\ref{Tab:compose_gsm8k_skill}. The handcrafting effort involved here is comparable to other few-shot prompting approaches such as CoT.
We mainly compare SKiC with zero/few-shot standard prompting \citep{brown2020language}, CoT~\citep{wei2022chain}, Least-to-Most (LtM) \citep{zhou2022least}, and Decomp \citep{khot2022decomposed} on different LLMs including LLAMA \citep{touvron2023llama1}, GPT3 (text-davinvi-003) \citep{brown2020language}, ChatGPT and GPT4~\citep{openai2023gpt4}. For tasks in the second setting, we further compare our methods with Scratchpad \citep{nye2021show}, Learning-to-Program (LtP) \citep{guo2023learning},  ComplexCoT \citep{fu2022complexity} and ensemble strategies such as majority voting (maj1@k) \citep{lewkowycz2022solving}, Self-Consistency (SC) \citep{cot_wei_sc}, Progressive-Hint Prompting (PHP) \citep{zheng2023progressive}, and Code-based-Verification (CSV)\citep{zhou2023solving}. Note that all the exemplars in SKiC are either a subset of or the same as what have been used in baselines. 
\subsection{Near-Perfect Systematic Generalization}
We report the main results for last letter concatenation, addition \& multiplication, Commaqa-E and DP in Figures~\ref{fig:main_results}-\ref{fig:QA_results}. Standard zero/few-shot prompting generalizes poorly on the problems that are harder than the exemplars in the prompting context. 
CoT, LtM and Decomp improve the overall performance but still degrade quickly over harder inputs. SKiC significantly boosts the performance in harder cases. 
Notably, SKiC achieves nearly perfect generalization on tasks like last letter concatenation, addition, and dynamic programming with text-davinci-003, ChatGPT or GPT4. 
These significant improvements highlight the importance of in-context skills and explicit grounding in eliciting compositionality.
Examples of the generated answers with SKiC can be found in Figures~\ref{Tab:example_last_letter_skill}-\ref{Tab:example_compose_dp_skill}.


