\title{Skills-in-Context Prompting:  Unlocking Compositionality in Large Language Models}

\begin{abstract}This study investigates methods for eliciting compositional generalization in large language models (LLMs). Compositional generalization refers to the ability to combine foundational skills to solve complex problems. We examine this within the framework of in-context learning. The results indicate that demonstrating both foundational skills and compositional examples grounded in those skills within the same prompt context is a critical factor. This prompt structure is termed skills-in-context (SKiC). With as few as two exemplars, this structure enables LLMs to solve more challenging problems requiring novel skill combinations, achieving near-perfect systematic generalization across a range of tasks. SKiC also appears to facilitate more active utilization of pre-existing internal skills acquired during pretraining to solve complex reasoning problems. The SKiC structure is robust across different skill constructions and exemplar choices and demonstrates transferability to new tasks. Finally, based on the in-context learning findings, we show that fine-tuning LLMs with SKiC-style data can elicit zero-shot weak-to-strong generalization, enabling models to solve harder problems with standard prompting.\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong performance on many natural language processing tasks (smith2022using, lewkowycz2022solving, wei2021finetuned, mishra2022cross, chung2022scaling, ouyang2022training, openai2023gpt4, touvron2023llama). Scaling model and data size has been associated with improved zero/few-shot performance, as characterized by scaling laws (kaplan2020scaling, hoffmann2022training) and emergent abilities (wei2022emergent). However, LLMs still struggle with compositional generalization, defined as the ability to use existing skills to solve more complex, unseen problems (zhou2022least, dziri2023faith, burns2023weak).

If an LLM has learned a set of foundational skills, it should, in principle, be able to solve problems whose solutions are composable from those skills. A key challenge is teaching LLMs how to use these skills to construct solutions. Several in-context learning strategies have been developed to improve reasoning and composition capabilities. Chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting (wei2022chain) improves reasoning performance by demonstrating a sequence of steps. Follow-up methods like Least-to-Most prompting (zhou2022least) and decomposed prompting (khot2022decomposed) employ a two-stage strategy: first decomposing a problem into sub-problems, then solving and combining them sequentially. While these methods improve performance on many compositional tasks, they often fail on problems significantly harder than those seen in the exemplars. Furthermore, Least-to-Most and decomposed prompting are restricted to problem classes that can be decomposed into a sequential chain of sub-problems. For problems with general computation graphs (dziri2023faith), constructing prompting exemplars is less intuitive and may not be possible.

This paper examines how to elicit compositional generalization in LLMs within the in-context learning framework. We find that a key factor is teaching the LLM to explicitly ground each reasoning step on more foundational skills. To achieve this, it is crucial to demonstrate both the foundational skills and compositional examples grounded in those skills within the same prompt context. We refer to this one-stage prompting structure as skills-in-context (SKiC).

Specifically, the SKiC prompt consists of three main blocks (Figure 1). The first block contains a short list of skills, including their instructions, that may be needed to solve a complex problem. These skills can be distilled manually or automatically via LLMs. The second part consists of a few exemplars (generally two) demonstrating how to compose skills into a complex solution. The last part is the testing problem.

When both skills and their explicit compositions are presented in context, LLMs learn to ground reasoning steps onto mastered skills, yielding stronger generalization. This approach allows LLMs to achieve near-perfect systematic generalization across a range of tasks. It also allows LLMs to generalize beyond the skills provided in the context, solving problems by more actively and explicitly using internal skills acquired during pretraining. This demonstrates that the SKiC structure facilitates synergies between skills and composition capabilities, enabling generalization to unseen, harder problems requiring innovative skill compositions. Furthermore, the SKiC structure is robust across different skill constructions (e.g., handcrafted or discovered by LLMs) and exemplar choices and demonstrates transferability to new tasks. Finally, inspired by the in-context learning study, we show that fine-tuning LLMs with SKiC-style data can elicit zero-shot weak-to-strong generalization, enabling models to solve much harder problems directly with standard prompting.\section{SKiC: Elicit Compositionality with In-Context Skills and Grounding}

\label{sec: methodology}
While humans naturally exhibit compositional generalization in problem-solving, LLMs often struggle to compose \textit{basic skills} to solve more difficult problems \citep{dziri2023faith}. Empowering LLMs with the ability to compose skills that they have seen to solve more complex tasks is important to mirror human intelligence and to reach superintelligence. In this work, we investigate how to elicit compositionality of LLMs in in-context learning (ICL) setting. In particular, we want to reveal how a meticulously designed prompt structure could greatly enhance the compositional ability. The insights obtained in the ICL setting can also inspire how to further improve the fine-tuning (Sec. \ref{sec:ift}).

\paragraph{Demonstration of Composition}
We find that it is crucial to instruct the LLM to explicitly ground each of its reasoning steps onto the foundational skills\footnote{``Foundational skills'' are not necessarily atomic. Rather, they could be any skills (e.g., a composite skill by itself) that serve as the building blocks for tackling complex problems.}. To facilitate this, it is important to demonstrate both the foundational skills and the compositional examples grounded in these skills within the same prompt context. Such a structure, which we refer to as SKiC, provides a full-context demonstration of how to perform \emph{explicit} composition over skills for solving a (complex) problem, where the detailed three-part construction is illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:example} as we discussed earlier. It is also partly inspired by the Elaborative Rehearsal from the human cognition theory \citep{berry1983metacognitive}, where studies \citep{kheirzadeh2016depth} have demonstrated that by first summarizing relevant knowledge and skills as the Scaffolding \citep{hammond2005putting} and establishing connections between the problem-solving steps and the existing Scaffolding, human would process the new information with greater depth and thoroughness, thus reinforcing both the concepts and their practical applications \citep{bakker2015scaffolding}. Our ablation study in Table \ref{Tab:different_components_main} will reveal that both the in-context skills and the explicit groundings are essential for eliciting strong compositional abilities.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\begin{center}
\centerline{\includegraphics[width=2.0\columnwidth]{example_math.png}}
\caption{An example of the generated solution on the MATH task using SKiC. Intriguingly, the two highlighted skills $<$Angle Bisector Theorem$>$ and $<$Heron's Formula$>$ are neither provided in the SKiC context (see Figure \ref{Tab:math_skill}) nor used in any given exemplars. LLMs harness the internal skills in their pre-trained knowledge to solve the problem, where these two highlighted skill names are also generated automatically by the LLM.} \label{Tab:example_compose_math_skill_5}
\end{center}
\vskip -0.2in
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Comparison to existing approaches}
Different from Chain-of-Thoughts, our SKiC provides explicit grounding on the foundational skills at each of the reasoning steps and also provides the relevant skills within the same context. Compared to recent prompting methods for handling compositional problems such as Least-to-Most (LtM) \citep{zhou2022least} and Decomp \citep{khot2022decomposed}, our SKiC is superior in several dimension: (i) Our SKiC is more general to solve extended sets of problems. Previous decomposing-based approaches like LtM and Decomp usually solve complex problems in a two-stage fashion by first decomposing the problem into a linear sequence of subproblems and then solving them sequentially. However, many of the tasks that have complex computation graphs such as multiplication and dynamic programming problems~\citep{dziri2023faith} cannot be decomposed in a simple manner, which makes these decomposition-based approaches less applicable. (ii) The decomposition operation can also be viewed as one basic skill in SKiC (see Figure~\ref{Tab:qa_skill} for an example in a question-answer task). (iii) SKiC solves the complex problems in a single stage, which could alleviate the error propagation compared to decomposition-based approaches that require multiple distinct stages. Due to the one-stage nature, our SKiC can replace other one-stage strategies such as the CoT in a plug-and-play manner. And it can be easily combined with other ensemble techniques such as self-consistency \citep{cot_wei_sc} and Progressive-Hint \citep{zheng2023progressive} to further boost the performance. 

\paragraph{Construction of the skills} \label{Sec:skic_construction}
One important component in the above SKiC structure is the foundational skills. Note that these skills are not meant to be an exclusive coverage over all the necessary skills. Instead, they are intended to be used together with the compositional exemplars to demonstrate how to perform explicit and grounded composition. For this reason, we only need a limited number of in-context skills since they only need to be used together with a few (typically $2 \sim 10$) compositional exemplars. Therefore, the human effort involved in constructing these skills are generally minimal or at most comparable to other few-shot prompting approaches. Indeed, our experimental analysis shows that SKiC requires less number of demonstration examples.
\paragraph{Grounding the composition}
As shown in Figure \ref{fig:example}, we explicitly ground the reasoning steps onto the corresponding skills in the compositional exemplars. Intriguingly, with SKiC, the LLMs can more actively tap into the vast reservoir of the internal skills they acquired during the pre-training stage in complex reasoning. In Figure \ref{Tab:example_compose_math_skill_5}, we demonstrate an example of the generated solution on the MATH task using SKiC. The two highlighted skills $<$Angle Bisector Theorem$>$ and $<$Heron's Formula$>$ are neither provided in the SKiC context (see Figure \ref{Tab:math_skill}) nor used in any given exemplars. LLMs automatically ground onto the (pre-trained) internal skills and compose them in their output reasoning steps. Notably, these two highlighted skill names are also automatically generated by the LLM.




